fact WorkItem(id: "WI-768", description: some(value: "Dispatch still matches parameterized binding keys by RAW SYMBOL IDENTITY, so it now disagrees with the typer. SITE: match_candidate_against_goal (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, fn at 12591) -- raw base guard `if c_base != p_base` at 12643, then `p_bindings.iter().find(|(kk, _)| kk == k)` at 12649 with `None => return false`; a second raw key lookup at 12818. SAME DEFECT CLASS AS WI-764: a same-base guard followed by a raw-identity binding-key lookup, where the two producers spell one slot differently -- a relation VALUE from a rule citation keys T as the canonical `anthill.prelude.Relation.T`, a WRITTEN signature keys it as a bare `T` (loader `reintern(p.last())`). WHY THIS IS NOW WORSE THAN BEFORE: WI-764 fixed the SUBTYPE relation to accept that pair but deliberately left dispatch alone, so the two are ASYMMETRIC -- a call can type-check and then fail to dispatch. On a key miss this site does not degrade, it returns false and DROPS the candidate, surfacing as a spurious DotDispatchNoMatch / ambiguity naming an impl that visibly should have matched, with no hint that a key merely failed to line up. NOT A FREE FOLLOW-UP, which is why WI-764 did not do it: `*specificity = specificity.saturating_add(1)` at 12647 means enrolling changes overload ORDERING, not only acceptance. NOT DEMONSTRATED -- no repro was constructed; the asymmetry is read off the code plus WI-764s own measured producer split, so step one is to build one (a spec op over a `Relation[T = .., E = ..]` carrier, called with a relation cited from a rule). FIX SHAPE: route both lookups through the shared `binding_for_param` / `binding_index_for_param` with `BindingKeyMatch::for_bases`, and consider widening the base guard from raw `!=` to `same_sort_canonical` to match. ACCEPTANCE: a repro that type-checks but fails to dispatch, pinned as a regression test; both lookups enrolled through the shared helper; the overload-ORDERING effect of the specificity bump MEASURED (e.g. instrument candidate ranking across the corpus) rather than assumed harmless; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T06:14:36Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-768", author: "user", content: "CODE-REVIEW findings recorded, not fixed here (each is outside the demonstrated defect):\n\n(1) DUPLICATE KEY SPELLINGS CAN DOUBLE-MATCH. binding_for_param is first-wins, so with the Label fallback two distinct candidate keys naming ONE slot can both resolve to the SAME per-call binding, leaving another per-call binding never compared. In values_structurally_equal the ab.len() != bb.len() arity guard does NOT catch it (2 == 2); match_candidate_against_goal arm (2) has no length check at all. Not hypothetical: WI-764's DuplicateParam guard is scoped to a Sort head only, and WI-764 itself recorded unroll_annotation_with_inferred building Relation[T=.., E=.., E=..] from ordinary source. Fixing it means tracking consumed indices in the SHARED helper, which changes WI-726/WI-764 consumers too.\n\n(2) values_structurally_equal NEVER COMPARES THE UNWRAPPED BASES. Its guard is sort_sym_of_term(a) == sort_sym_of_term(b), which yields the SortView WRAPPER symbol for a SortView-encoded value, so SortView(List,[T=Int64]) and SortView(Stream,[T=Int64]) pass the guard; parametric_value_parts then unwraps to List vs Stream and for_bases correctly returns Identity, both key T identically, and the function returns TRUE for two values of DIFFERENT sorts. A one-line same_sort_canonical(ab_base, bb_base) closes it and the full suite stays green (3091/0) with it in — but green only proves it does not break anything, not that it ever fires, and reachability is unclear (impl_subst stores only per-call values). REVERTED rather than ship a second unmeasurable tightening in a function whose WI-768 enrollment is already undemonstrated.\n\n(3) goals_equal IS A FOURTH HAND-ROLLED SPELLING of the binding-key rule. It does NOT carry the WI-768 bug (already same_label, so it bridges the pair), but it is label-ONLY: it lacks the identity-first pass that makes an exact key beat a merely same-labelled one, and it puts same_label's debug_assert on the path unconditionally. Its spec_sort guard already establishes Label, so enrolling it is a one-liner — deferred because changing its tie-breaking is a behavior change outside this ticket. NOW DOCUMENTED in binding_for_param's un-enrolled list, which previously undercounted (said 'one site').", at: "2026-07-19T06:12:46Z")
